To set up Arduino CLI on Windows 10 for the first time, follow these steps:

1. Install Python: Download Python 3.x from the official Python website and install it on your computer. Make sure to add Python to your system's PATH during the installation process.

2. Install Arduino CLI: Open a command prompt (cmd) and type the following command:
   ```
   pip install arduino-cli
   ```
   This will install Arduino CLI.

3. Install Board Support Package (BSP) for your ESP-WROOM-32: Download the BSP package from the official Arduino website or GitHub. Extract the package and place it in a directory of your choice.

4. Configure Arduino CLI: To configure the Arduino CLI, open a command prompt and type the following command:
   ```
   arduino-cli config init
   ```
   This will generate a configuration file in your home directory (~/.arduino15/arduino-cli.txt). Edit the file and add the following lines:
   ```
   board=esp32:esp-wroom-32
   core=esp32
   toolchain-bin.xtensa-esp32-gnu-toolchain=C:\path\to\esp-2021r2-win32\bin\
   tools.xtensa-esp32-elf=C:\path\to\esp-2021r2-win32\bin\xtensa-esp32-elf-gcc.exe
   ```
   Replace "C:\path\to\esp-2021r2-win32\" with the path to your ESP-IDF installation directory.

5. Install Libraries: To install libraries, use the following command:
   ```
   arduino-cli lib install <library-name>
   ```
   Replace "<library-name>" with the name of the library you want to install. Some useful libraries for ESP32 are:
   - ArduinoJson: Used for parsing and generating JSON data.
   - ESPAsyncWiFiManager: Used for managing Wi-Fi connections.
   - ESPAsyncTCP: Used for creating TCP/IP sockets and handling TCP/IP communication.
   - ESPAsyncWebServer: Used for creating web servers.

