To modify your program so that it asks the user whether they want to terminate the program when a `SIGINT` (Ctrl+C) or `SIGTERM` signal is received, you can use a signal handler function. This function will be called when either of these signals is caught by your program. In the signal handler, you can prompt the user and depending on their input, decide whether to exit the program or continue execution.

Below is your program modified to include a signal handler for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>

#define NUM_THREADS 4
#define NUM_LINES 100
#define NUMBERS_PER_LINE 50
#define MAX_LINE_LENGTH 256

sem_t *semaphore;
int global_sum = 0;
int lines_read_by_thread[NUM_THREADS] = {0};
char lines[NUM_LINES][MAX_LINE_LENGTH];
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
volatile sig_atomic_t ask_to_terminate = 0; // Used to handle signal-based termination

void signal_handler(int signum) {
    if (!ask_to_terminate) { // Ask only once
        char response[4];
        printf("\nDo you want to terminate the program? (yes/no): ");
        if (fgets(response, sizeof(response), stdin) && strncmp(response, "yes", 3) == 0) {
            exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
        } else {
            printf("Continuing execution...\n");
            ask_to_terminate = 1; // Prevent asking again
        }
    }
}

void setup_signal_handling() {
    struct sigaction sa;
    sa.sa_handler = signal_handler; // Set signal_handler as the handler function
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
    sa.sa_flags = 0;
    sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL);
    sigaction(SIGTERM, &sa, NULL);
}

// generate_random_data and reader_thread functions are unchanged

int main() {
    setup_signal_handling(); // Setup signal handling
    srand(time(NULL));
    // The rest of main() remains unchanged
}
```

**Key Changes and Notes:**

1. **Signal Handling Setup:** The `setup_signal_handling` function sets up the signal handling for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` using `sigaction`. It specifies `signal_handler` as the function to be called when these signals are caught.

2. **Signal Handler:** The `signal_handler` function checks if it's the first time the user is being asked to terminate the program (using `ask_to_terminate`). If so, it prompts the user. If the user responds with "yes", the program exits. Otherwise, it sets `ask_to_terminate` to 1 to prevent asking again and continues execution.

3. **Global `volatile sig_atomic_t` Variable:** The `ask_to_terminate` variable is marked as `volatile sig_atomic_t`. This is important for variables accessed within a signal handler and the main program to ensure they are not optimized out by the compiler and that their access is atomic.

Please note that using I/O functions like `printf` and `fgets` inside signal handlers is generally not recommended because they are not async-signal-safe. For educational purposes and simple scenarios, this might work, but for production-level code, consider using safer alternatives or designing the program flow to minimize the use of such functions within signal handlers.

