{
    // Create a new window
    sf::RenderWindow window(sf::VideoMode(400, 400), "Smiling Face");

    // Create a circle for the face
    sf::CircleShape face(100);
    face.setFillColor(sf::Color::Yellow);
    face.setPosition(100, 100);

    // Create circles for the eyes
    sf::CircleShape leftEye(20);
    leftEye.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);
    leftEye.setPosition(140, 150);

    sf::CircleShape rightEye(20);
    rightEye.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);
    rightEye.setPosition(240, 150);

    // Create a semi-circle for the smiling mouth using a convex shape
    sf::ConvexShape smile;
    smile.setPointCount(4);
    smile.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);
    smile.setPoint(0, sf::Vector2f(150, 230));
    smile.setPoint(1, sf::Vector2f(250, 230));
    smile.setPoint(2, sf::Vector2f(220, 260));
    smile.setPoint(3, sf::Vector2f(180, 260));

    // Run the program as long as the window is open
    while (window.isOpen())
    {
        // Check all the window's events that were triggered since the last iteration of the loop
        sf::Event event;
        while (window.pollEvent(event))
        {
            // "Close requested" event: we close the window
            if (event.type == sf::Event::Closed)
                window.close();
        }

        // Clear the window with white color
        window.clear(sf::Color::White);

        // Draw everything
        window.draw(face);
        window.draw(leftEye);
        window.draw(rightEye);
        window.draw(smile);

        // End the current frame
        window.display();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code sets up a new window and draws a smiling face using shapes provided by SFML. The `sf::RenderWindow` object represents the window, and we use `sf::CircleShape` objects for the face and eyes. The mouth is created with an `sf::ConvexShape` to approximate a semi-circle.

Remember that this code requires linking against the SFML libraries. If you're using an IDE like Visual Studio, Code::Blocks, or a build system like CMake, make sure to configure your project to link against the SFML graphics, window, and system components.

Compile and run the code, and a window should open displaying a simple smiling face. If you encounter any issues, ensure that SFML is correctly installed and linked in your project settings.

