Great! Let's break this process down into several steps:

1. **Setting up the environment**: Before you begin, make sure you have Node.js and npm installed on your machine. You can check this by running `node -v` and `npm -v` in your terminal. If you don't have them installed, you can download Node.js and npm from [here](https://nodejs.org/en/download/). 

2. **Creating a new React project**: You can create a new React application using the create-react-app command line tool. Run this command in your terminal:

    ```
    npx create-react-app webxr-app
    ```
   This will create a new folder in your current directory called `webxr-app`, which contains all the files you need to start a React application.

3. **Installing Three.js**: Three.js is a JavaScript library used to create and display animated 3D computer graphics in a web browser. You can add it to your project with this command:

    ```
    npm install three
    ```

4. **Installing WebXR API**: WebXR is a group of standards which are used together to support rendering and input for Virtual Reality (VR) and Augmented Reality (AR) on the web. Unfortunately, there isn't a specific npm package for it, you have to use the native API provided by the browsers. 

5. **Installing MUI** (formerly Material-UI): MUI is a simple and customizable component library to build faster, beautiful, and more accessible React applications. Follow the [installation guide](https://mui.com/getting-started/installation/) on the official MUI documentation.

6. **Building the Application**: Now that you have all your dependencies installed, you can start building your application. You'll want to create your 3D graphics with Three.js and use the WebXR API to display them in VR or AR. With MUI, you can style your application to your liking. 

Remember, there isn't a standard way to combine these technologies as it depends heavily on what you're trying to achieve with your application. However, the main idea would be to create your 3D models and animations with Three.js, then use React to manage your state and render your components, and finally, style everything with MUI.

For more details and examples on how to use these libraries, you can check out their official documentation:
- [Three.js documentation](https://threejs.org/docs/)
- [WebXR API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API)
- [MUI documentation](https://mui.com/)

Here's a simple example of how your `index.js` file might look. This example doesn't include WebXR or Three.js setup, but it demonstrates how to set up a basic React application with MUI (formerly Material-UI). 

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import { CssBaseline } from '@mui/material';
import { ThemeProvider, createTheme } from '@mui/material/styles';
import App from './App';

const theme = createTheme({
  palette: {
    primary: {
      main: '#556cd6',
    },
    secondary: {
      main: '#19857b',
    },
    error: {
      main: '#red',
    },
    background: {
      default: '#fff',
    },
  },
});

ReactDOM.render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <CssBaseline />
      <App />
    </ThemeProvider>
  </React.StrictMode>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```
In this file, we're creating a MUI theme and providing it to the application via the `ThemeProvider`. We're also including MUI's `CssBaseline` to add some nice default styles. The `App` component, which isn't defined here, would be your main application component.

To integrate Three.js and WebXR, you'd want to create separate components for your 3D and XR content, and render them within the `App` component or wherever else they're needed. The specifics of this will depend on what you're trying to achieve with your application.

Remember, the WebXR API isn't available as an npm package, but rather as a native API provided by the browser. So you'll need to check for its availability and use it directly in your components. The Three.js library can be imported normally using `import * as THREE from 'three';`.

Please note, this is a very basic setup and there is no unique way to structure your application as it heavily depends on your specific needs and preferences.

