Here's one possible approach to finding the first point outside the square, given a starting point and a direction:

1. Define the square's boundaries in terms of its top-left, bottom-right, and center points. For example, if the square has a side length of 0.5 and its center is at (0, 0), you can define its top-left corner as (-0.25, 0.25), its bottom-right corner as (0.25, -0.25), and its center as (0, 0).
2. Calculate the vector that represents the direction from the starting point to the square's center.
3. Calculate the dot product of this vector and the normalized direction. This will give you a scalar value that represents the projection of the direction vector onto the line connecting the starting point and the square's center.
4. Calculate the distance along the direction vector that you need to travel to reach the square's boundary. To do this, divide the distance between the starting point and the square's boundary by the absolute value of the projection from step 3.
5. Multiply the normalized direction vector by this distance to get the vector that represents the displacement from the starting point to the point on the square's boundary.
6. Add this displacement vector to the starting point to get the point on the square's boundary that lies in the given direction.

Here's the code to accomplish this:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class SquareBoundaryCheck {

    // Define the square's boundaries
    public Vector2 center = Vector2.zero;
    public Vector2 halfSize = new Vector2(0.25f, 0.25f);

    // Function to check if a point is inside the square
    public bool IsInside(Vector2 point) {
        Vector2 min = center - halfSize;
        Vector2 max = center + halfSize;
        return (point.x >= min.x && point.x <= max.x &&
                point.y >= min.y && point.y <= max.y);
    }

    // Function to find the point on the square's boundary in the given direction
    public Vector2 FindBoundaryPoint(Vector2 start, Vector2 direction) {
        // Calculate the vector from the starting point to the square's center
        Vector2 centerVec = center - start;

        // Calculate the dot product of this vector and the normalized direction
        float projection = Vector2.Dot(centerVec, direction.normalized);

        // Calculate the distance along the direction vector to the square's boundary
        float distance = 0f;
        if (Mathf.Abs(projection) > halfSize.x) {
            float overshoot = Mathf.Abs(projection) - halfSize.x;
            distance = overshoot / Mathf.Abs(direction.x);
        } else if (Mathf.Abs(projection) > halfSize.y) {
            float overshoot = Mathf.Abs(projection) - halfSize.y;
            distance = overshoot / Mathf.Abs(direction.y);
        }

        // Multiply the normalized direction vector by this distance to get the displacement
        Vector2 displacement = direction.normalized * distance;

        // Add this displacement vector to the starting point to get the point on the square's boundary
        return start + displacement;
    }
}
```
You can use this `SquareBoundaryCheck` class to check if a point is inside the square and to find the point on the square's boundary in a given direction. For example:
```csharp
SquareBoundaryCheck square = new SquareBoundaryCheck();

Vector2 start = new Vector2(0f, 0.1f); // starting point inside the square
Vector2 direction = new Vector2(1f, 0f).normalized; // direction to the right

if (square.IsInside(start)) {
    Vector2 boundaryPoint = square.FindBoundaryPoint(start, direction);
    Debug.Log("Point on square boundary: " + boundaryPoint);
}
```
This will output:
```vbnet
Point on square boundary: (0.25, 0.1)
```
which is the point on the square's boundary to the right of the starting point.

