Creating a smooth animation effect of changing an SVG's color using HTML and CSS can be achieved using CSS keyframes and transitions. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Define the SVG element in your HTML file:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>SVG Color Animation</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <svg class="animation-target" width="100" height="100">
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="black" stroke-width="3" fill="red" />
    </svg>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a CSS file (e.g., `styles.css`) to define the keyframes and apply the transition:
```css
@keyframes colorChange {
  0% {
    fill: red;
  }
  50% {
    fill: yellow;
  }
  100% {
    fill: blue;
  }
}

.animation-target {
  animation: colorChange 5s infinite;
  transition: fill 3s;
}
```
In the above example, the `colorChange` animation keyframes define the color transitions from red to yellow to blue. The `animation` property is applied to the SVG element with the class `animation-target`, and it runs the `colorChange` animation infinitely for 5 seconds. The `transition` property is also applied to the SVG element, and it defines the duration of the fill color transition as 3 seconds.

This setup will create a smooth animation effect that changes the SVG's color from red to yellow to blue, and then repeats the process. You can adjust the duration of the animation, the color stops, and the transition duration to fit your requirements.

