Sure, here's a Go program that solves this problem. The program reads the input from a file named `input.txt`. It uses a stack to process the polymer, pushing characters onto the stack unless the top of the stack is a character of the same type but opposite polarity, in which case both characters are removed.

```go
package main

import (
	"io/ioutil"
	"log"
	"unicode"
)

func main() {
	data, err := ioutil.ReadFile("input.txt")
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	polymer := string(data)
	stack := make([]rune, 0)

	for _, unit := range polymer {
		if len(stack) > 0 && unicode.ToLower(stack[len(stack)-1]) == unicode.ToLower(unit) && stack[len(stack)-1] != unit {
			stack = stack[:len(stack)-1] // pop the stack
		} else {
			stack = append(stack, unit) // push onto the stack
		}
	}

	log.Printf("After all possible reactions, %v units remain", len(stack))
}
```
This program reads the input file, then iterates over each character in the polymer. It checks if the current character matches the character at the top of the stack in type but not in polarity. If so, it pops the stack. Otherwise, it pushes the current character onto the stack. The size of the stack at the end of the program is the number of units remaining in the polymer after all reactions.

