Here is a concise and efficient Golang program to solve this task:

```go
package main

import (
    "bufio"
    "fmt"
    "os"
)

func react(polymer string) string {
    for i := 0; i < len(polymer)-1; i++ {
        if polymer[i] != polymer[i+1] && 
           (polymer[i]+32 == polymer[i+1] || 
            polymer[i]-32 == polymer[i+1]) {
            return react(polymer[:i] + polymer[i+2:]) 
        }
    }
    return polymer
}

func main() {
    file, _ := os.Open("input.txt")
    scanner := bufio.NewScanner(file)

    scanner.Scan()
    polymer := scanner.Text()

    result := react(polymer)
    fmt.Println(len(result))
}
```

The key aspects:

- Read input from file line-by-line
- Define a `react` function that reduces the polymer by removing adjacent opposite units
- Recursively call `react` until no more reactions possible
- Print out length of resulting polymer

This avoids having to copy the polymer string in memory for each reaction.

