for (unsigned int I = 0; I < 32; i++) {
    count += (var >> i) & 1;
}

return count; // return the number of set bits in the original variable
```

To find the number of set bits in a 32 bit variable `num`, you can first create a copy of `num` with half of the bits set (which can be done efficiently using a pattern such as `0x55555555`), then iterate over each bit position and increment a counter for each set bit. The inner expression `(var >> i) & 1` efficiently extracts the value of the bit at position `i` (by shifting `var` right by `i` bits and then masking with 1).

This implementation has a time complexity of O(32) or O(1) per variable, making it very efficient for finding the number of set bits in a 32 bit integer.

