Here's a solution in Kotlin to this problem:

```kotlin
fun mergeAndSort(a: IntArray, b: IntArray): Int {
    val merger = Merger(a, b)
    val sortedA = merger.mergeAndSort()
    val sortedB = merger.mergeAndSort()

    if (sortedA == null || sortedB == null) {
        return -1
    }

    return 0
}

class Merger(private val a: IntArray, private val b: IntArray) {
    private val aIndex = 0
    private val bIndex = a.size
    private val combined = mutableListOf<Int>()
    private val aAndB = listOf(a, b)

    private fun mergeAndSort(): IntArray? {
        val result = IntArray(a.size + b.size)
        var index = 0

        while (combined.size < result.size) {
            val (nextA, nextB) = when {
                aIndex < a.size && bIndex < b.size -> Pair(a[aIndex], b[bIndex]) to intArrayOf(aIndex + 1, bIndex + 1)
                aIndex < a.size -> Pair(a[aIndex]) to intArrayOf(aIndex + 1, bIndex)
                bIndex < b.size -> Pair(b[bIndex]) to intArrayOf(aIndex, bIndex + 1)
                else -> return null
            }

            val (value, (na, nb)) = nextA
            val (va, vb) = when {
                value < aAndB[0][na] -> Pair(va = value, vb = nextA.second)
                value == aAndB[0][na] -> Pair(va = value, vb = aAndB[1][nb])
                else -> Pair(va = nextA.first, vb = value)
            }

            result[index] = va
            index++
            combined[na] = value

            aIndex += va == aAndB[0][na]
            bIndex += vb == aAndB[1][nb]
        }

        return result
    }
}
```

The time complexity of this solution is O(n log n) for each of the arrays, where n is the size of the arrays. The total time complexity is O(2n log n), which simplifies to O(n log n).

The space complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the size of the arrays. This is because we are using additional arrays and lists to store the merged and sorted elements.

Please note that this solution assumes that the input arrays are non-empty and contain only integer values. If this is not the case, you may need to modify the solution accordingly.

