Here is an example of a PHP SQL insert query that inserts data into a table named "my\_table" in a MySQL database:
```
<?php

// Connect to the database
$conn = mysqli_connect("localhost", "username", "password", "database_name");

// Check if the connection was successful
if (!$conn) {
    die("Connection failed: " . mysqli_connect_error());
}

// Create a new array with the data to insert
$data = array(
    "column1" => "value1",
    "column2" => "value2",
    // Add additional columns and values as needed
);

// Prepare the insert statement
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($conn, "INSERT INTO my_table (column1, column2, ...) VALUES (?, ?, ...)");

// Bind the data to the statement parameters
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "ss", $param1, $param2);

// Set the parameters
$param1 = $data['column1'];
$param2 = $data['column2'];
// Add additional parameters as needed

// Execute the statement
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);

// Close the statement and the connection
mysqli_stmt_close($stmt);
mysqli_close($conn);

?>
```
This example uses the `mysqli_connect` function to connect to the MySQL database, and the `mysqli_prepare` function to prepare an insert statement. It then binds the data to the statement parameters using the `mysqli_stmt_bind_param` function, and sets the parameters using the `$data` array. Finally, it executes the statement using the `mysqli_stmt_execute` function and closes the statement and the connection using the `mysqli_stmt_close` and `mysqli_close` functions, respectively.

Note that you will need to replace "localhost", "username", "password", and "database\_name" with the appropriate values for your MySQL server and database. You will also need to replace "column1", "value1", "column2", and "value2" with the appropriate column and value names for your table.

