Certainly! Here are two basic commands to rename an index in an Oracle database using SQL:

1. Using the `ALTER INDEX` statement:
```sql
ALTER INDEX old_index_name RENAME TO new_index_name;
```
Replace `old_index_name` with the name of the index you want to rename, and `new_index_name` with the new name you want to give to the index.

2. Using the `EXECUTE` statement with the `DBMS_REDEFINITION` package:
```sql
DECLARE
  l_old_index_name VARCHAR2(30) := 'old_index_name';
  l_new_index_name VARCHAR2(30) := 'new_index_name';
BEGIN
  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'BEGIN DBMS_REDEFINITION.START_INDEX_RENAME(OBJECT_NAME => ''' || l_old_index_name || ''', INDEX_NAME => ''' || l_old_index_name || ''', NEW_NAME => ''' || l_new_index_name || '''); END;';
  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'BEGIN DBMS_REDEFINITION.FINALIZE_INDEX_RENAME(OBJECT_NAME => ''' || l_old_index_name || ''', INDEX_NAME => ''' || l_old_index_name || ''', NEW_NAME => ''' || l_new_index_name || '''); END;';
END;
```
Replace `old_index_name` with the name of the index you want to rename, and `new_index_name` with the new name you want to give to the index. Note that this method requires the `ALTER` system privilege and the `DBMS_REDEFINITION` package to be available.

Both methods will rename the index in the Oracle database, but you should choose the one that best fits your requirements and the privileges you have.

