Certainly! Here's a simple example of a React server component that renders a "Hello, World!" message:
```jsx
import React from'react';

const HelloWorld = (props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
    </div>
  );
};

export default HelloWorld;
```
And here's how you can use this component in a server-side rendered application using Node.js and Express:
```jsx
import React from'react';
import HelloWorld from './HelloWorld';

const App = (props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{props.title}</h1>
      <HelloWorld {...props} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```
In this example, the `App` component takes a `title` prop which is passed down to the `HelloWorld` component as a prop. The `App` component is rendered using the ReactDOM server library:
```jsx
import React from'react';
import ReactDOMServer from'react-dom/server';
import App from './App';

const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const title = 'Node.js';
  const props = { title: title };
  ReactDOMServer.renderToString(<App {...props} />, (err, html) => {
    res.send(html);
  });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```
That's it! This example demonstrates how to create a server-side rendered component using React, and how to use it in a Node.js application.

