Yes, it is possible to automatically detect the layout of an HDF5 file and create corresponding structs in a C++ program using the HighFive library.

HighFive provides a variety of tools for working with HDF5 files, including the `h5::File` class, which can be used to open and read data from an HDF5 file. The `h5::File` class provides methods for reading data from different layout formats, such as the `read_data()` method, which can be used to read data from the file into a user-defined struct or data array.

To automatically detect the layout of an HDF5 file, you can use the `h5::File::get_layout()` method to obtain the layout object for the file, and then use the `h5::LayoutGetPlugin()` method to get a list of all available layout plugins for the layout object.

Once you have a list of available layout plugins, you can use the `h5::LayoutPlugin::read()` method to read data from the file into a user-defined struct or data array, using the appropriate layout plugin.

Here is an example of how you can automatically detect the layout of an HDF5 file and read data into a struct:
```
#include <h5.h>
#include <iostream>

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <hdf5_file>" << std::endl;
        return 1;
