Here's an example of an automation program in SCL (Structured Control Language) for a PLC (Programmable Logic Controller) that takes input from a sensor called I0.1 and turns on an output called O0.1:

```scl
PROGRAM MYPROGRAM

INPUTS:
  I0.1;

OUTPUTS:
  O0.1;

VARIABLES:
  VAR_NAME: BOOL;

ALARMS:
  ALARM_NAME(MSG): ALARM(MSG);

PROCESSBLOCK:
  IF I0.1 THEN
    O0.1 := TRUE;
    VAR_NAME := TRUE;
  ELSE
    O0.1 := FALSE;
    VAR_NAME := FALSE;
  END_IF;

ALARM(ALARM_NAME(MS1)):
  IF VAR_NAME = TRUE THEN
    DISPLAY("ALARM: " + MS1);
  END_IF;

END_PROGRAM(MYPROGRAM);
```

In this example, we define the inputs, outputs, variables, and alarms for the program. The input is the sensor I0.1, and the output is O0.1. We also define a variable called VAR_NAME that will be used to store the status of the input.

In the process block, we use an IF statement to check the status of the input I0.1. If it's TRUE, we set the output O0.1 to TRUE and also set the variable VAR_NAME to TRUE. If the input is FALSE, we set the output O0.1 to FALSE and also set VAR_NAME to FALSE.

We also define an alarm called ALARM_NAME with a message called MS1. If the variable VAR_NAME is TRUE, we display a message when the alarm is triggered.

