The process of ordering a pizza can be broken down into a series of steps that can then be modeled in a UML activity diagram. Here's a simple breakdown of the process:

1. Decide to order pizza
2. Choose pizza toppings
3. Choose pizza size
4. Choose delivery or pickup
5. Provide delivery address (if delivery chosen)
6. Provide contact information
7. Confirm order
8. Make payment
9. Receive order confirmation
10. Wait for pizza to arrive (if delivery chosen)
11. Receive pizza
12. Eat pizza

Now, let's translate this process into PlantUML code that can be used to render an activity diagram in a PlantUML editor. 

```plantuml
@startuml
title Ordering a Pizza

start

:Decide to order pizza;
:Choose pizza toppings;
:Choose pizza size;
:Choose delivery or pickup;
if (Delivery?) then (yes)
  :Provide delivery address;
else (no)
endif
:Provide contact information;
:Confirm order;
:Make payment;
:Receive order confirmation;
if (Delivery?) then (yes)
  :Wait for pizza to arrive;
  :Receive pizza;
else (no)
  :Pick up pizza;
endif
:Eat pizza;

stop

@enduml
```

To render this diagram, you would simply copy and paste this code into a PlantUML editor, and the editor will generate the activity diagram based on the provided code.

Please note that this is a simplified model and real-world scenarios may involve more complex interactions such as choosing multiple toppings, dealing with payment authorization, handling different payment methods, or managing delivery tracking.

