Sure! Here's a simple Java 17 code example that demonstrates the use of the new 'sealed' classes feature:

```java
public sealed interface Animal permits Cat, Dog {
    void makeSound();
}

final class Cat implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

final class Dog implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal1 = new Cat();
        Animal animal2 = new Dog();

        animal1.makeSound(); // Output: Meow!
        animal2.makeSound(); // Output: Woof!
    }
}
```

In this example, we define an interface called 'Animal' using the 'sealed' modifier. The 'sealed' modifier restricts which classes can implement the interface. In this case, only the 'Cat' and 'Dog' classes are allowed to implement the 'Animal' interface.

The 'Cat' and 'Dog' classes both implement the 'Animal' interface and provide their own implementation of the 'makeSound()' method.

In the 'Main' class, we create instances of 'Cat' and 'Dog' and assign them to variables of type 'Animal'. We then invoke the 'makeSound()' method on these instances, which prints out different sounds based on the specific implementation.

This example demonstrates how the 'sealed' classes feature can be used to restrict the set of classes that can implement an interface, providing more control over the types that can be used in a certain context.

