Sure, I can help you with that. Here's a basic example of how you can build user authentication using NestJS.

First, you'll need to create a user module that will handle user-related functionalities. You can create a user service that will be responsible for handling the logic for user authentication.

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  private users = [
    { id: 1, username: 'user1', password: 'password1' },
    { id: 2, username: 'user2', password: 'password2' },
  ];

  async findByUsername(username: string): Promise<any> {
    return this.users.find(user => user.username === username);
  }

  async validateUser(username: string, password: string): Promise<any> {
    const user = await this.findByUsername(username);
    if (user && user.password === password) {
      const { password, ...result } = user;
      return result;
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

Next, you can create an auth service that will handle the authentication logic.

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { UserService } from './user.service';

@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  constructor(private userService: UserService) {}

  async validateUser(username: string, password: string): Promise<any> {
    return this.userService.validateUser(username, password);
  }
}
```

Then, you can create an auth module to handle the authentication routes.

```typescript
import { Module } from '@nestjs/common';
import { AuthService } from './auth.service';
import { UserModule } from './user.module';

@Module({
  imports: [UserModule],
  providers: [AuthService],
})
export class AuthModule {}
```

Finally, you can use the `AuthGuard` and `JwtModule` provided by NestJS to add authentication to your routes.

This is a basic example to get you started. You can further enhance the authentication logic by adding features like token-based authentication, role-based access control, and more.

