In Jinja templates, you can refer to a user-defined Enum in the same way you would refer to any other variable. First, you need to pass the Enum to the template. Then, you can access the Enum values directly.

Here's an example:

Python code:
```python
from enum import Enum
from flask import render_template

class Color(Enum):
    RED = 1
    BLUE = 2
    GREEN = 3

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return render_template('home.html', Color=Color)
```

Jinja template(home.html):
```html
{% if some_variable == Color.RED.value %}
    <p>The color is red.</p>
{% elif some_variable == Color.BLUE.value %}
    <p>The color is blue.</p>
{% elif some_variable == Color.GREEN.value %}
    <p>The color is green.</p>
{% else %}
    <p>Unknown color.</p>
{% endif %}
```

In this case, `Color` is the Enum you defined, and `some_variable` is a variable that you are comparing to the Enum values. `Color.RED.value` will give you the actual value of the Enum member (in this case `1`), and you can compare it to your variable.

Remember to pass the Enum to the template in your view function (`render_template('home.html', Color=Color)`), otherwise it won't be accessible in the template.

