Sure, I can write a simple implementation to multiply two 8x8 matrices in Galois Field 2 (GF(2)). Here is a simple C++ function that does this:

```C++
#include <vector>
#include <bitset>

std::vector<char> multiplyMatricesGF2(std::vector<char>& A, std::vector<char>& B) {
    std::vector<char> C(8, 0); // Initialize result matrix with 0s

    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) {
            std::bitset<8> row(A[i]); // Convert char to a bitset for easier manipulation
            std::bitset<8> col;
            for(int k = 0; k < 8; ++k){ // Create column bitset
                col[k] = (B[k] >> j) & 1;
            }
            std::bitset<8> product = row & col; // Perform AND operation
            int sum = product.count() % 2; // Count 1s and modulo 2
            C[i] |= (sum << j); // Set the bit in the result matrix
        }
    }
    return C;
}
```
This function works by iterating over each cell in the result matrix, and for each cell, it computes the dot product of the corresponding row from matrix A and column from matrix B. The dot product is computed in GF(2), meaning that each multiplication is replaced with an AND operation, and each addition is replaced with an XOR operation (which is equivalent to counting the number of 1s and taking modulo 2).

