To determine if a game you're running on Windows is CPU bound, you can follow these steps:

1. Open the Task Manager by pressing Ctrl + Shift + Esc or by right-clicking on the taskbar and selecting "Task Manager."

2. In the Task Manager, go to the "Performance" tab.

3. Look at the CPU usage graph. If the CPU usage is consistently high (close to 100%) while running the game, it indicates that the game is utilizing a significant amount of CPU resources.

4. You can also check the CPU usage of the game specifically. Switch to the "Processes" or "Details" tab in the Task Manager and look for the game's process. Check the CPU usage percentage for the game process. If it is consistently high, it suggests that the game is CPU bound.

Additionally, you can use third-party software like MSI Afterburner or HWiNFO to monitor CPU usage and performance in real-time. These tools provide more detailed information about CPU utilization and can help identify if the game is primarily limited by CPU performance.

Keep in mind that if a game is CPU bound, it means that the CPU is the bottleneck and may be limiting the game's performance. In such cases, upgrading the CPU or optimizing the game's settings may be necessary to achieve better performance.

You are correct. In cases where a game is single-thread bound, the overall CPU usage may not be near 100% because only one CPU core or thread is being heavily utilized, while the other cores may have lower usage.

To determine if a game is single-thread bound, you can follow these steps:

1. Open the Task Manager by pressing Ctrl + Shift + Esc or by right-clicking on the taskbar and selecting "Task Manager."

2. In the Task Manager, go to the "Performance" tab.

3. Look at the CPU usage graph. If the CPU usage for a specific core or thread is consistently high (close to 100%) while running the game, it suggests that the game is heavily relying on a single thread.

4. You can also check the CPU usage of the game process specifically. Switch to the "Processes" or "Details" tab in the Task Manager and look for the game's process. Check the CPU usage percentage for the game process. If it is consistently high on a specific core or thread, it indicates that the game is single-thread bound.

Monitoring software like MSI Afterburner or HWiNFO can provide more detailed information about individual core/thread usage, which can help identify if a game is primarily limited by a single thread.

If a game is single-thread bound, it means that the game's performance is being limited by the performance of a single core or thread. In such cases, improving the single-thread performance of your CPU or optimizing the game's settings may be necessary to achieve better performance.

Certainly! Here's an example of C++ code using the Windows API to detect CPU bound threads:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <Windows.h>
#include <Psapi.h>
#include <vector>

// Function to check if a thread is CPU bound
bool IsThreadCPUBound(DWORD threadId)
{
    HANDLE hThread = OpenThread(THREAD_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION, FALSE, threadId);
    if (hThread == NULL)
    {
        std::cerr << "Failed to open thread: " << threadId << std::endl;
        return false;
    }

    FILETIME creationTime, exitTime, kernelTime, userTime;
    if (!GetThreadTimes(hThread, &creationTime, &exitTime, &kernelTime, &userTime))
    {
        std::cerr << "Failed to get thread times for thread: " << threadId << std::endl;
        CloseHandle(hThread);
        return false;
    }

    ULONGLONG kernelTimeMs = (static_cast<ULONGLONG>(kernelTime.dwHighDateTime) << 32) | kernelTime.dwLowDateTime;
    ULONGLONG userTimeMs = (static_cast<ULONGLONG>(userTime.dwHighDateTime) << 32) | userTime.dwLowDateTime;

    CloseHandle(hThread);

    // Threshold to determine if a thread is CPU bound
    const ULONGLONG cpuBoundThresholdMs = 500; // Adjust this value based on your requirements

    // Check if both kernel time and user time exceed the threshold
    return kernelTimeMs > cpuBoundThresholdMs && userTimeMs > cpuBoundThresholdMs;
}

int main()
{
    DWORD processId = GetCurrentProcessId();
    HANDLE hProcess = OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ, FALSE, processId);
    if (hProcess == NULL)
    {
        std::cerr << "Failed to open process: " << processId << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Get the process's thread list
    DWORD threadIds[1024];
    DWORD threadCount = GetProcessIdOfThread(hProcess);
    if (!EnumThreadWindows(threadIds, sizeof(threadIds), &threadCount))
    {
        std::cerr << "Failed to enumerate threads for process: " << processId << std::endl;
        CloseHandle(hProcess);
        return 1;
    }

    std::vector<DWORD> cpuBoundThreads;

    // Iterate through the threads and check if they are CPU bound
    for (DWORD i = 0; i < threadCount; i++)
    {
        DWORD threadId = threadIds[i];
        if (IsThreadCPUBound(threadId))
        {
            cpuBoundThreads.push_back(threadId);
        }
    }

    CloseHandle(hProcess);

    // Print the CPU bound threads
    if (cpuBoundThreads.empty())
    {
        std::cout << "No CPU bound threads found." << std::endl;
    }
    else
    {
        std::cout << "CPU bound threads: ";
        for (const DWORD threadId : cpuBoundThreads)
        {
            std::cout << threadId << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Please note that this code uses the Windows API functions `OpenThread`, `GetThreadTimes`, `OpenProcess`, and `EnumThreadWindows` to retrieve the thread information and CPU times. Make sure to link against `Kernel32.lib` and `Psapi.lib`. Also, adjust the `cpuBoundThresholdMs` value as per your requirements to determine what threshold constitutes a CPU bound thread.

